WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:02.669
Cosmic horror--it isn't
the typical scary stuff

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:05.922
about a fear of dying or evoking
some kind of physical terror,

00:00:05.922 --> 00:00:08.675
but an existential crisis
made manifest--

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:11.511
the idea that we as humans
have no purpose whatsoever,

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:14.347
essentially making our lives,
our very existence,

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:16.933
meaningless,
turned into a monster.

00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:18.977
H.P. Lovecraft is often cited

00:00:18.977 --> 00:00:21.521
as the originator,
or at least popularizer,

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:23.022
of cosmic horror.

00:00:23.022 --> 00:00:26.109
In all of his fictional texts,
he explores the theme,

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:28.695
resulting in some
pretty terrifying plots,

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:30.655
like in At the Mountain
of Madness,

00:00:30.655 --> 00:00:33.408
where humanity's existence
is revealed to be

00:00:33.408 --> 00:00:36.453
an accidental result
of a scientific experiment

00:00:36.453 --> 00:00:37.871
by aliens.

00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:40.206
Then there are
his original monsters.

00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:43.710
There are fungal beings
who appear as winged, clawed

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.503
carapace-covered creatures;

00:00:45.503 --> 00:00:49.382
shapeless, protoplasmic bubbles
made of iridescent black slime;

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:51.050
and the most notorious--

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:54.888
a tentacled cosmic giant,
Cthulhu.

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:57.599
Cthulhu is the Old One's
high priest

00:00:57.599 --> 00:00:59.642
who can help humans
liberate themselves

00:00:59.642 --> 00:01:01.644
from the constructs
of morality

00:01:01.644 --> 00:01:04.731
to "shout and kill and revel
and enjoy themselves

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:08.276
in a destructive revelry
'of ecstasy and freedom.'"

00:01:08.276 --> 00:01:10.528
Lovecraft may be
Cthulhu's inventor,

00:01:10.528 --> 00:01:13.406
but the creation has outgrown
its originator,

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:16.242
impacting both the horror genre
and real life

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:17.744
in significant ways.

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.370
[theme music]

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:23.082
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,
and this is "Monstrum."

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:25.585
Cthulhu's head
is a pulpy mass

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:28.922
of tentacle-like feelers
resembling an octopus.

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:32.717
Its rubbery, grotesque body
is covered in scales.

00:01:32.717 --> 00:01:35.303
Its back has
rudimentary narrow wings,

00:01:35.303 --> 00:01:38.932
and long talons adorn
its hind and fore feet.

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:41.392
Described as
part dragon, part octopus,

00:01:41.392 --> 00:01:44.604
and part human,
this monster is so bizarre,

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:48.525
so terrifying, it drives
anyone who sees it mad.

00:01:48.525 --> 00:01:50.985
In Lovecraft's universe,
Cthulhu is a leader

00:01:50.985 --> 00:01:53.571
or holy figure
akin to a priest

00:01:53.571 --> 00:01:55.615
and one of the Great Old Ones--

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:57.992
an extraterrestrial species
that inhabited Earth

00:01:57.992 --> 00:02:00.537
long before humans
called it home.

00:02:00.537 --> 00:02:03.998
The Old Ones and Cthulhu
aren't really gods, per se--

00:02:03.998 --> 00:02:06.751
after all,
"God" is a human construction--

00:02:06.751 --> 00:02:10.463
but they serve as symbols
of unknowable cosmic forces.

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:14.175
While the Old Ones are no longer
physical beings on Earth,

00:02:14.175 --> 00:02:15.385
they cannot die,

00:02:15.385 --> 00:02:17.679
so calling them
dead doesn't quite work.

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:20.056
They lie awake in the dark,
ever-watching,

00:02:20.056 --> 00:02:23.768
hidden under the Earth's crust
and beneath the ocean's depths

00:02:23.768 --> 00:02:25.645
and can still be contacted.

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:27.689
They are considered
a lost culture,

00:02:27.689 --> 00:02:29.941
although some parts
of Lovecraft's world

00:02:29.941 --> 00:02:31.901
continue to worship
the creatures.

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:33.903
The Old Ones continue
to communicate

00:02:33.903 --> 00:02:35.655
with humans telepathically

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:38.116
and can be physically evoked
through rituals,

00:02:38.116 --> 00:02:41.661
but only if the astral
alignments are just right.

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:44.455
The notorious Cthulhu
first rose from the depths

00:02:44.455 --> 00:02:46.833
of the ocean from
a sunken city called R'lyeh

00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:50.003
in Lovecraft's short story,
"The Call of Cthulhu,"

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:53.006
originally published
in the February, 1928,

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:54.841
pulp magazine "Weird Tales."

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:57.427
"The Call of Cthulhu"
is told from the perspective

00:02:57.427 --> 00:03:00.346
of narrator
Francis Wayland Thurston

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.307
who investigates
the strange records left

00:03:02.307 --> 00:03:04.767
by his recently deceased
great uncle.

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:06.519
In the story
that started the legacy,

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:10.189
Cthulhu is never encountered
physically by a character,

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:12.525
seen only in dreams
and artwork.

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:14.694
Lovecraft's inspiration
for the story

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:17.071
came years earlier
in a dream about a man

00:03:17.071 --> 00:03:19.616
exploring a museum
of antiquities.

00:03:19.616 --> 00:03:21.701
When Lovecraft
returned to the idea,

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:23.828
he wove in elements
from his current life

00:03:23.828 --> 00:03:25.455
in Providence, Rhode Island,

00:03:25.455 --> 00:03:28.875
including real addresses and
even a documented earthquake.

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:32.003
He also found inspiration
in pre-existing literature,

00:03:32.003 --> 00:03:34.380
like the invisible
mind-manipulating presence

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.593
at the center of French author
Guy de Maupassant's The Horla,

00:03:38.593 --> 00:03:40.428
as well as A. Merritt's
The Moon Pool,

00:03:40.428 --> 00:03:42.722
Arthur Machen's
The Three Imposters,

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:46.392
and W. Scott-Elliot's
Atlantis and the Lost Lemuria.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:48.895
I also have to mention
that Lovecraft wrote

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:52.398
extensively about how much he
hated eating seafood and fish,

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:55.568
which makes those tentacles
and horror-from-the-ocean themes

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:57.403
seem a lot more intentional.

00:03:57.403 --> 00:04:00.406
Lovecraft grew up during the
second Industrial Revolution,

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:03.576
when the increased presence
of railroads, automobiles,

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.453
electrical power,
telephones,

00:04:05.453 --> 00:04:07.747
and other
rapid industrial advancements

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:10.500
contributed to urbanization,
commercialism,

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.418
and cultural development
all around,

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:14.921
not to mention
environmental impacts.

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:18.049
Lovecraft asked his readers
to consider what would happen

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:21.678
if our impact was taken away
or made meaningless.

00:04:21.678 --> 00:04:24.347
For fans of Lovecraft today,
it may come as a surprise

00:04:24.347 --> 00:04:28.559
that for nearly two decades
after his death in 1937,

00:04:28.559 --> 00:04:31.479
he was widely dismissed
as an untalented writer

00:04:31.479 --> 00:04:33.898
by critics
and public audiences alike.

00:04:33.898 --> 00:04:35.817
His works were viewed
as nothing more

00:04:35.817 --> 00:04:38.319
than fodder for young adults
and eccentrics,

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:41.406
although some would argue
similar sentiments today.

00:04:41.406 --> 00:04:43.241
Born in Providence in 1890,

00:04:43.241 --> 00:04:45.952
his father died of syphilis
in a mental asylum

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:48.454
and he was raised by his mother
and maternal family members

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:49.998
from a young age.

00:04:49.998 --> 00:04:53.251
While intelligent--he was
an avid reader at age three--

00:04:53.251 --> 00:04:55.420
he was also
a high-school drop-out.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:59.132
His literary career
began in 1914 as a journalist,

00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:01.634
although he quickly expanded
into fiction.

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:03.553
His influences were vast,

00:05:03.553 --> 00:05:06.389
and included gothic literature,
historical travelogues,

00:05:06.389 --> 00:05:08.683
Greek classics,
Romantic literature,

00:05:08.683 --> 00:05:11.144
scientific discoveries
of the 19th century,

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:14.647
and his own agnostic beliefs
and ancestral history.

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:16.691
After a failed marriage
in his 30s--

00:05:16.691 --> 00:05:18.401
their separation
partially a result

00:05:18.401 --> 00:05:20.737
of his xenophobic
and anti-Semitic beliefs--

00:05:20.737 --> 00:05:22.322
his wife, Sonia,
was the daughter

00:05:22.322 --> 00:05:25.366
of Jewish-Russian immigrants--
he lived with his aunts.

00:05:25.366 --> 00:05:28.661
His own published work
never produced a livable income.

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:31.998
He used his inheritance
and skills as a copy editor

00:05:31.998 --> 00:05:33.708
and ghostwriter to survive.

00:05:33.708 --> 00:05:36.461
Lovecraft's lifetime
marked a period of transition

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:38.046
in the United States,

00:05:38.046 --> 00:05:40.923
as the country attempted
to come into a cohesive identity

00:05:40.923 --> 00:05:42.383
amid the first world war,

00:05:42.383 --> 00:05:44.427
changing views of sex
and women's rights,

00:05:44.427 --> 00:05:47.513
the spread of socialism,
new scientific discoveries,

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:50.224
and increasingly booming
immigrant population.

00:05:50.224 --> 00:05:53.561
All of these themes and
the anxieties surrounding them

00:05:53.561 --> 00:05:54.937
are present in his work.

00:05:54.937 --> 00:05:57.231
Despite some of these
now-outdated opinions,

00:05:57.231 --> 00:06:00.109
Lovecraft's influence
on other writers and creators

00:06:00.109 --> 00:06:02.070
of all kinds is extensive,

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:04.864
enough so that it can be
a challenge to even locate

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:06.699
all the references
to his works,

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:10.369
especially because Lovecraft
used overlapping narratives--

00:06:10.369 --> 00:06:13.081
sticking references
to his own creations

00:06:13.081 --> 00:06:14.874
into the texts
he was ghostwriting

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:16.209
and encouraging other authors

00:06:16.209 --> 00:06:18.419
to reference his universe
in their own.

00:06:18.419 --> 00:06:21.214
Books, characters, towns,
and even monsters

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:24.050
appear in-world,
as a collective tradition

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:26.677
commonly called
the Cthulhu Mythos.

00:06:26.677 --> 00:06:29.430
The term Cthulhu Mythos
was coined by Lovecraft's

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.307
author friend,
August Derleth,

00:06:31.307 --> 00:06:33.351
the first person
to collect and publish

00:06:33.351 --> 00:06:35.686
Lovecraft's short stories
in one volume

00:06:35.686 --> 00:06:37.730
under the Arkham House
publishing company.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:41.067
Some argue that the success
of Lovecraft's Cthulhu Mythos

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:43.402
is at least in part
made possible

00:06:43.402 --> 00:06:45.571
by the habitable worlds
he created.

00:06:45.571 --> 00:06:48.741
Stories were often set
in seemingly realistic spaces--

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:50.576
New England towns,
for instance--

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:53.830
whose physical characteristics,
character genealogies,

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.541
and histories
are crafted with such detail

00:06:56.541 --> 00:06:58.376
they feel real
to the reader.

00:06:58.376 --> 00:07:01.796
Even Cthulhu possesses
just enough recognizability.

00:07:01.796 --> 00:07:03.840
It is
a non-supernatural monster

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.384
who is both god
and extraterrestrial,

00:07:06.384 --> 00:07:07.677
but its appearance contains

00:07:07.677 --> 00:07:10.096
enough physical
real-world markers,

00:07:10.096 --> 00:07:11.973
like the octopus comparisons,

00:07:11.973 --> 00:07:14.767
that is existence
seems plausible...

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:17.436
but still very, very scary.

00:07:17.436 --> 00:07:19.438
Cthulhu is an Eldritch being--

00:07:19.438 --> 00:07:21.858
a weird, eerie,
sinister force.

00:07:21.858 --> 00:07:23.693
The etymology of the word
"Eldritch"

00:07:23.693 --> 00:07:25.862
can be traced
back to monsters--

00:07:25.862 --> 00:07:27.196
elves, more specifically.

00:07:27.196 --> 00:07:29.240
Originating
in the Scottish language,

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:32.243
the term applies to those things
that resemble or suggest

00:07:32.243 --> 00:07:34.620
elves or supernatural beings,

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.123
invocating associations
with the weird, the foreign,

00:07:37.123 --> 00:07:39.542
the strange,
and the uncanny.

00:07:39.542 --> 00:07:41.836
Cthulhu is
considered extraterrestrial

00:07:41.836 --> 00:07:43.713
and no doubt a monster.

00:07:43.713 --> 00:07:46.591
Cthulhu and the Great Old Ones
force humanity

00:07:46.591 --> 00:07:48.801
to reconsider their place
in the world.

00:07:48.801 --> 00:07:52.513
Lovecraft's creatures are not
malevolent, just indifferent,

00:07:52.513 --> 00:07:53.931
at least to humans.

00:07:53.931 --> 00:07:56.601
Some scholars and fans
even argue that this is

00:07:56.601 --> 00:07:59.437
where much of the horror
of Lovecraft comes from.

00:07:59.437 --> 00:08:03.357
We are disturbed by the fact
that not only are we inferior,

00:08:03.357 --> 00:08:05.651
but that the beings
older and wiser than us

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:08.613
simply don't care
about our existence.

00:08:08.613 --> 00:08:11.240
The concept of Eldritch
post-H.P. Lovecraft

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:13.993
is inherently tied
to what is generally believed

00:08:13.993 --> 00:08:16.913
to be a modern invention--
cosmic horror.

00:08:16.913 --> 00:08:18.289
This unique form of spooky

00:08:18.289 --> 00:08:20.666
is an extension
of the gothic and nihilism,

00:08:20.666 --> 00:08:23.252
where terror arrives
from that which reminds us

00:08:23.252 --> 00:08:25.713
that we not only
cannot comprehend

00:08:25.713 --> 00:08:27.215
everything in the universe,

00:08:27.215 --> 00:08:29.759
but that those things
are inherently bad.

00:08:29.759 --> 00:08:31.344
Part of Cthulhu's history

00:08:31.344 --> 00:08:33.596
is outlined in
the Necronomicon--

00:08:33.596 --> 00:08:34.931
an invented mythical work

00:08:34.931 --> 00:08:38.059
supposedly penned by one
of Cthulhu's devotees

00:08:38.059 --> 00:08:41.395
that Lovecraft references
in many of his stories.

00:08:41.395 --> 00:08:44.398
It outlines the origins
of the Old Ones.

00:08:44.398 --> 00:08:46.859
Lovecraft initially claimed
the Necronomicon

00:08:46.859 --> 00:08:49.737
was translated
from a real-world Arabic text--

00:08:49.737 --> 00:08:52.448
a playful allusion
to the romantic gothic genre

00:08:52.448 --> 00:08:54.492
where other fictional
origins of texts

00:08:54.492 --> 00:08:56.035
became a common trope.

00:08:56.035 --> 00:08:58.621
He would later admit
that the Necronomicon text

00:08:58.621 --> 00:09:00.039
never existed.

00:09:00.039 --> 00:09:03.209
Lovecraft himself called it
"synthetic folklore."

00:09:03.209 --> 00:09:05.628
Keep in mind,
Lovecraft's original texts

00:09:05.628 --> 00:09:08.047
that included mention
of the Necronomicon

00:09:08.047 --> 00:09:09.924
are intentionally vague.

00:09:09.924 --> 00:09:13.010
While some passages have been
incorporated into his stories,

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:16.764
there was no full manuscript
anyone could base a book on,

00:09:16.764 --> 00:09:18.349
so people made one up.

00:09:18.349 --> 00:09:19.642
In the 1970s,

00:09:19.642 --> 00:09:21.269
physical copies
of the Necronomicon

00:09:21.269 --> 00:09:24.522
were published by an author
under the pseudonym of "Simon,"

00:09:24.522 --> 00:09:27.233
although it borrowed more
from Sumerian mythology.

00:09:27.233 --> 00:09:30.778
Hoax versions of the text
still pop up from time to time.

00:09:30.778 --> 00:09:33.197
Considered a religious text,
a cult book,

00:09:33.197 --> 00:09:35.700
or demonic grimoire
depending on the audience,

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:38.828
Lovecraft's entirely fictional
Book of the Dead

00:09:38.828 --> 00:09:42.748
became a real-world participant
in the Cthulhu mythos.

00:09:42.748 --> 00:09:45.167
There are real people
who worship Cthulhu,

00:09:45.167 --> 00:09:47.378
and even crimes have
been committed in the name

00:09:47.378 --> 00:09:49.588
of the so-called religious text.

00:09:49.588 --> 00:09:51.674
Not all of
Lovecraft's influences

00:09:51.674 --> 00:09:53.301
are so serious, however.

00:09:53.301 --> 00:09:55.594
The Necronomicon
plays a key role

00:09:55.594 --> 00:09:57.263
in the "Evil Dead" franchise,

00:09:57.263 --> 00:09:59.056
pops up in "Jason Goes to Hell,"

00:09:59.056 --> 00:10:01.100
and appears in many comics.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:04.478
In the 1980s, Lovecraft
and his most famous creations

00:10:04.478 --> 00:10:06.981
were introduced
to a much wider audience

00:10:06.981 --> 00:10:09.275
with the production
of the role-playing game

00:10:09.275 --> 00:10:10.776
called "Cthulhu,"

00:10:10.776 --> 00:10:13.362
which was basically about
your character going insane.

00:10:13.362 --> 00:10:16.574
Video games have also been based
on Cthulhu mythos.

00:10:16.574 --> 00:10:19.744
There are even kid-friendly
versions of Cthulhu,

00:10:19.744 --> 00:10:22.663
like plushies, baby books,
and school gear.

00:10:22.663 --> 00:10:25.249
The cute interpretations
literally soften

00:10:25.249 --> 00:10:26.959
the monster's grotesqueness

00:10:26.959 --> 00:10:30.171
and encourage playful
or even protective behavior

00:10:30.171 --> 00:10:31.797
rather than existential terror.

00:10:31.797 --> 00:10:35.134
And I for one would love to know
how the author would feel

00:10:35.134 --> 00:10:36.469
about cute Cthulhu,

00:10:36.469 --> 00:10:38.637
especially given
its commodification.

00:10:38.637 --> 00:10:40.181
While Lovecraft is considered

00:10:40.181 --> 00:10:43.142
a prolific and significant
author of the 20th century,

00:10:43.142 --> 00:10:45.978
it is undeniable
that much of his fiction

00:10:45.978 --> 00:10:47.980
reflects his real-world racism.

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:49.690
While his xenophobia
might have been

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:51.817
more acceptable
during his lifetime,

00:10:51.817 --> 00:10:53.819
it's jarring
for the modern reader.

00:10:53.819 --> 00:10:55.696
Modern texts
like Lovecraft Country,

00:10:55.696 --> 00:10:58.282
Ring Shout,
and The Ballad of Black Tom,

00:10:58.282 --> 00:11:00.993
deal with the racist
and misogynist tensions

00:11:00.993 --> 00:11:02.453
in his texts head-on,

00:11:02.453 --> 00:11:04.789
crafting new additions
to the Cthulhu mythos

00:11:04.789 --> 00:11:07.333
that are still applicable
to our world today.

00:11:07.333 --> 00:11:10.961
Humanity has come a long way
from what the world looked like

00:11:10.961 --> 00:11:12.463
when Lovecraft was alive,

00:11:12.463 --> 00:11:15.174
but the contemplation
of our role in the universe,

00:11:15.174 --> 00:11:16.467
or lack thereof,

00:11:16.467 --> 00:11:19.178
is still at the forefront
of our minds.

00:11:20.054 --> 00:11:21.305
History lovers,

00:11:21.305 --> 00:11:23.307
we need to tell you
about a new series

00:11:23.307 --> 00:11:25.226
from PBS
called "The Bigger Picture."

00:11:25.226 --> 00:11:27.019
Hosted by
Professor Vincent Brown,

00:11:27.019 --> 00:11:29.814
it's a show that examines
famous photographs

00:11:29.814 --> 00:11:32.650
and unpacks the historical
context around them.

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:35.528
In doing so, we reveal
what these photos can tell us

00:11:35.528 --> 00:11:39.156
about our history,
but also how we view ourselves.

00:11:39.156 --> 00:11:41.700
Check out the first episode
at the link in the description

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:43.786
and head
to the PBS YouTube channel

00:11:43.786 --> 00:11:44.954
to see it for yourself.

00:11:44.954 --> 00:11:47.331
Tell them "Monstrum" sent you.

00:11:47.331 --> 00:11:49.708
Lovecraft's original texts
that included mention

00:11:49.708 --> 00:11:51.419
of the Necronomonicon--

00:11:51.419 --> 00:11:53.546
Why am I changing up
the vowels?

00:11:53.546 --> 00:11:57.258
In the 1970s, physical copies
of the Necronomo--

